WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:09.010
- Hello Class of 2020.

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:11.850
These past couple of
months I, like most of you,

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:14.840
have found myself feeling
nostalgic for all the moments

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:17.630
that have made my time
at Yale feel so special.

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:20.140
But as comforting as
it can be to reminisce,

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:23.550
we must also open our hearts
and minds to what lies ahead,

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:25.680
and today we get to do both.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:28.300
We, on the Class Day Committee,
are very excited to share

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.600
with you the Class History slide show,

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:33.320
a compilation of photos
submitted by all of you

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:36.740
commemorating our journeys
as undergraduates.

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:39.620
But before we start cringing
at pictures from Yale Up,

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:42.360
I have the honor of
introducing Dean Marvin Chun,

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:45.170
who will offer insight into
our individual and collective

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:47.883
futures as the unique class of 2020.

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:52.550
- Class of 2020, congratulations.

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:55.830
I am so happy to be with
you today, even by video,

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:57.720
and to celebrate with you.

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:00.380
Graduating is a great
accomplishment, one of the most

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:02.130
important ones of your life.

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:04.430
And now that this moment has finally come,

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:06.720
I am honored to recognize it with you.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:08.940
I also recognize all the
work that you have done

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:11.430
to get you here, much
of it with the support

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:13.290
of your family and friends.

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:15.843
Today is your day and it is theirs too.

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:19.770
Over the past week, together
with you, I've enjoyed

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:22.540
watching this series of Class Day videos.

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:26.090
Tomorrow there will be a toast
to the Yale Class of 2020,

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:27.920
like what many of you have celebrated

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:30.130
in your residential colleges.

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:32.870
On Sunday, I'm excited
for our Class Day speaker,

00:01:32.870 --> 00:01:37.380
Dr. Jean Bennett, a world-renowned
scientist and alumna.

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:39.650
Also this Sunday, prizes will be announced

00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:42.400
on the Yale2020 website.

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:45.120
I'm grateful to the Class Day
Committee and to the speakers

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:48.920
who all contributed to this
Class Week series of videos.

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:51.760
And I'm most appreciative
to you, especially now,

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:53.620
during senior week.

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:56.670
I miss seeing you on campus,
and I can't wait to welcome you

00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:58.760
back to campus in due time.

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:00.880
We've already started
planning all the parties

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:04.480
and ceremonies that we will
host for you and your families.

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:07.740
So until we meet in person,
please stay safe and well.

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:09.923
Again, Class of 2020, congratulations.

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:13.860
- Thank you, Dean Chun,
for your encouraging words.

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:16.370
And now let's take a moment
to look back at some of your

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:19.020
favorite memories from the past few years.

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:21.143
Please enjoy the Class History slide show.

00:02:23.082 --> 00:02:25.665
(upbeat music)

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:28.820
- All right, so first question.

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:31.320
How would you describe
yourself as a first year?

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:32.343
- Oh boy.

00:02:33.241 --> 00:02:35.824
(upbeat music)

00:02:45.587 --> 00:02:47.400
- I was too confident.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:50.810
- Naive, but well-intentioned.

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:53.210
- I was really eager to see
what Yale had in store for me

00:02:53.210 --> 00:02:57.400
for the next four years, but
I had no idea just how much

00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:58.233
that would be.

00:02:58.233 --> 00:03:00.350
- I'd say that I was a
little academically intense.

00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:02.950
- I was definitely extremely
caffeinated, probably,

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:03.950
at any given moment.

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:08.770
- I was energetic, enthusiastic,
ready to just dive in.

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:11.660
- I was always super
excited about everything,

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:14.710
but also a little lost
and confused constantly.

00:03:14.710 --> 00:03:16.823
- How'd I overcome sophomore slump?

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:19.270
I didn't really.

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:21.080
- Did I overcome sophomore slump?

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:23.470
- Does anyone truly
overcome sophomore slump?

00:03:23.470 --> 00:03:25.180
- Yeah, I don't know if I did either.

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:27.544
- Hit that and just went down from there.

00:03:27.544 --> 00:03:30.320
- Yale started to feel like
home when Ellie Odroit,

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:33.810
Lauren Telesz, Sam Issick,
and I committed to having

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:35.690
breakfast together every day.

00:03:35.690 --> 00:03:37.760
- When I really bought
into the Saybrook Community.

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:40.320
- I moved into my residential
college and really got

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.440
to know my suite and it
started feeling like a family.

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:45.650
- We threw our huge Christmas party.

00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:47.480
- I was on the phone
with my mom and she asked

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:49.420
where I was going, and
I said I was going home,

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:50.800
but I didn't mean to Texas.

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:53.440
I was just like walking
from the grocery store

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:55.680
back to my apartment in New Haven.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:00.223
- The last day of my first
year, when I had to say goodbye

00:04:00.223 --> 00:04:01.840
to my roommate and I was
like, we need to suck in,

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:03.580
this is all over.

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:05.360
- My favorite first event.

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:07.472
Freshman Olympics on Old Campus.

00:04:07.472 --> 00:04:10.170
(laughs)

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:11.900
- Definitely my first Spring Fling.

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:14.280
We did have good Spring Fling performers.

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:15.210
- Harvard - Yale game.

00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:16.316
- Harvard - Yale for sure.

00:04:16.316 --> 00:04:17.503
- Harvard - Yale.

00:04:17.503 --> 00:04:19.630
- Harvard - Yale was
unbelievably life-changing.

00:04:19.630 --> 00:04:23.200
- We won after having a
really long losing streak,

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:25.060
and no one thought it was going to happen.

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:28.300
It was so unexpected and
I'll never forget the last

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:30.590
few minutes of that game
ticking down when we realized

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:33.640
what was happening, and
everyone storms the field

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:35.032
at the end.

00:04:35.032 --> 00:04:37.615
(crowd cheers)

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:48.220
- The community that has been
most impactful on me at Yale

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:50.100
has definitely been the Morse's Community.

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:52.960
- Thai City and Sphinx, and Saybrook too.

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:55.500
- The Acapella community
and more specifically,

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:57.580
Mixed Company and the Whiffs.

00:04:57.580 --> 00:05:01.200
- Aurelian Honor Society, my
secret society has definitely

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:02.070
been there for me.

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:04.027
- Pearson College and the
Yale Ballroom Dance Team,

00:05:04.027 --> 00:05:05.580
of which I'm a proud member.

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:07.229
- I think we both have the
same answer for this one.

00:05:07.229 --> 00:05:08.292
- Yeah, we do.

00:05:08.292 --> 00:05:09.410
- It's probably the Dramat.

00:05:09.410 --> 00:05:11.230
- Definitely Dukes of Yale.

00:05:11.230 --> 00:05:12.820
Hands down.

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:13.653
Dukes.

00:05:13.653 --> 00:05:15.917
- [Girl] My favorite
memory is on Old Campus

00:05:15.917 --> 00:05:19.960
when it turned spring and
everyone emerges from their dorms

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:24.110
to picnic, that is the best,
like the first sunny day

00:05:24.110 --> 00:05:25.350
on Old Campus.

00:05:25.350 --> 00:05:28.850
- It was just beautiful weather
and Isaac's gonna be full,

00:05:28.850 --> 00:05:32.330
so a few, a couple of more,
Mark Rosenberg, Camille Beckford

00:05:32.330 --> 00:05:37.070
and myself threw around a
Frisbee, kinda for the first time

00:05:37.070 --> 00:05:38.140
since freshman year.

00:05:38.140 --> 00:05:42.140
- My friend, Sophia Zafurco,
she was doing office hours

00:05:42.140 --> 00:05:44.480
on Old Campus, which she
just like would sit outside

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:46.200
and play guitar and sing.

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:48.380
- I lived in Bingham, which was awesome,

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:50.830
but also I love being so
close to Harkness Tower

00:05:50.830 --> 00:05:53.480
and hot tink, I loved hearing
the bells ring every morning.

00:05:53.480 --> 00:05:55.370
- I have a lot of memories
of just sitting outside

00:05:55.370 --> 00:05:58.310
on the germ-free benches
for hours just talking

00:05:58.310 --> 00:05:59.710
to whoever passed by.

00:05:59.710 --> 00:06:01.510
- Just sitting down next to
someone that you don't know

00:06:01.510 --> 00:06:03.720
on a bench and introducing
yourself and just kind of that

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:05.570
mingling of people from
all different colleges.

00:06:05.570 --> 00:06:07.700
I made some friends that
lasted four years that way.

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:09.410
- Something that surprised
me about Yale, honestly,

00:06:09.410 --> 00:06:11.780
is just how quickly the years went by.

00:06:11.780 --> 00:06:14.030
I can't believe like we're
graduating right now.

00:06:14.030 --> 00:06:16.517
- Probably just how much
freedom Yale gives you.

00:06:16.517 --> 00:06:18.940
You can really kinda do whatever you want

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:21.470
with your Yale experience,
and the professors,

00:06:21.470 --> 00:06:24.040
the faculty will be there
to support you through it.

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:26.890
- Anyone would help you if you asked.

00:06:26.890 --> 00:06:28.860
And I think that's something
that's really special.

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:30.330
- Honestly, how nice people are.

00:06:30.330 --> 00:06:33.210
Like I feel like that's
something that we people just say

00:06:33.210 --> 00:06:35.410
all the time, like people
at Yale are so nice,

00:06:35.410 --> 00:06:37.000
but it's really true.

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:39.350
- Time is so short when
you're at Yale because there's

00:06:39.350 --> 00:06:41.180
just so much to do.

00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:44.820
There's a balance, but you
know, I wouldn't have had it

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:46.161
any other way.

00:06:46.161 --> 00:06:48.744
(upbeat music)

00:06:55.170 --> 00:06:58.700
- My favorite ice cream
spot on campus, Arethusa.

00:06:58.700 --> 00:06:59.533
- Arethusa.

00:06:59.533 --> 00:07:00.933
- Definitely Arethusa.

00:07:00.933 --> 00:07:03.020
- Arethusa, favorite
ice cream spot, easily.

00:07:03.020 --> 00:07:03.853
- Agreed.

00:07:03.853 --> 00:07:06.160
- Arethusa, butter pecan in a waffle cone.

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:09.360
- It was Ashley's for a long
time, but now it's Milk Craft.

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:11.460
- Ashley's, gotta get the Frisbee.

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:13.310
- Ashley's has the best
variety of flavors,

00:07:13.310 --> 00:07:16.520
but if you want richness and
creaminess, go to Arethusa.

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:18.853
- Favorite Yale dining dish.

00:07:19.690 --> 00:07:21.130
Gotta be the grilled cheese.

00:07:21.130 --> 00:07:22.743
The grilled cheese slaps.

00:07:24.090 --> 00:07:25.510
- I always go tenders.

00:07:25.510 --> 00:07:26.343
- [Girl] Chicken parm.

00:07:26.343 --> 00:07:28.870
- [Woman] If there are
multigrain pancakes on the menu,

00:07:28.870 --> 00:07:30.730
I will wake up just for those.

00:07:30.730 --> 00:07:33.573
- Finnish pancakes, and they're so good.

00:07:35.030 --> 00:07:36.160
I gotta learn how to make them.

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:37.340
- The stand-outs for me are always

00:07:37.340 --> 00:07:38.760
in the dessert department.

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:41.830
- Olive oil, pear, cardamom cake.

00:07:41.830 --> 00:07:44.950
I didn't even know olive oil
could be put in desserts,

00:07:44.950 --> 00:07:46.960
but it is the best thing I've ever eaten.

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:48.890
People are sleeping on this dessert.

00:07:48.890 --> 00:07:51.030
I've already made it like
three times in quarantine.

00:07:51.030 --> 00:07:53.227
It turned out terribly all three times,

00:07:53.227 --> 00:07:56.770
and I really wish that I could
have just one more slice.

00:07:56.770 --> 00:07:58.330
- Favorite coffee shop?

00:07:58.330 --> 00:08:01.040
Either Jojo's for the
honey lemon ginger tea,

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:02.630
or Book Traders for studying.

00:08:02.630 --> 00:08:04.120
- Definitely Blue State on York.

00:08:04.120 --> 00:08:06.100
- The Acorn in Silliman College.

00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:09.470
- Atticus, JoJo's, and Koffee? with a K.

00:08:09.470 --> 00:08:10.790
- Fussy Coffee, hands down.

00:08:10.790 --> 00:08:12.870
- Probably Willoughby's.

00:08:12.870 --> 00:08:13.860
Willoughby's does it right.

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:17.050
- Book Trader Cafe, the egg
and cheese changed my life.

00:08:17.050 --> 00:08:19.740
- Koffee? with a K, because
they have really good lemonade

00:08:19.740 --> 00:08:20.770
and I like lemonade.

00:08:20.770 --> 00:08:22.250
- Favorite New Haven restaurant?

00:08:22.250 --> 00:08:25.316
New Haven has a ton of
fantastic restaurants.

00:08:25.316 --> 00:08:26.149
- Rubamba or Zirafas.

00:08:26.149 --> 00:08:27.190
- Taste of China.

00:08:27.190 --> 00:08:28.736
- Rubamba or Olea.

00:08:28.736 --> 00:08:31.210
- Unias, specifically because
of the midnight grommet.

00:08:31.210 --> 00:08:32.200
- [Girl] Metro Noodle Bar.

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:33.453
- [Woman] Metro Noodle Bar.

00:08:33.453 --> 00:08:34.850
- I think Barcelona wins.

00:08:34.850 --> 00:08:35.683
- I love Yorkside.

00:08:35.683 --> 00:08:38.270
- If we're talking two
o'clock on a Friday,

00:08:38.270 --> 00:08:41.577
then probably Halal Guys
always hits the spot for me.

00:08:41.577 --> 00:08:44.470
- The best study spot for
me is actually in my bed.

00:08:44.470 --> 00:08:48.477
- If you are staying up late,
is the Pearson Dining Hall.

00:08:48.477 --> 00:08:50.517
- Music library.
- Murray Library.

00:08:50.517 --> 00:08:53.210
- An STI room with the
fireplace, where you can just

00:08:53.210 --> 00:08:56.820
put your feet up, read
a book next to the fire,

00:08:56.820 --> 00:08:57.690
look out the window.

00:08:57.690 --> 00:09:00.524
- In the Acorn on the study desks.

00:09:00.524 --> 00:09:03.107
(guitar music)

00:09:21.410 --> 00:09:23.300
- Biggest mentors at Yale.

00:09:23.300 --> 00:09:24.403
Oh my God.

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:29.020
- That's a big question.

00:09:29.020 --> 00:09:34.020
- JT, Professor Raymond, Tracy
from the Good Life Center,

00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:36.160
and Sissy from the Yale College office.

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:38.030
- Certainly the theater mentors.

00:09:38.030 --> 00:09:39.740
Actor Matt mentors for sure.

00:09:39.740 --> 00:09:41.063
- Reverend Jenny Peak,

00:09:42.580 --> 00:09:45.170
out here helping people navigate
queerness and Christianity

00:09:45.170 --> 00:09:46.003
all the time.

00:09:46.003 --> 00:09:47.530
- The actual like senior students

00:09:47.530 --> 00:09:50.460
in the student club, Yale Launch.

00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:51.710
They had founded their own company

00:09:51.710 --> 00:09:53.830
and they were really big mentors for me.

00:09:53.830 --> 00:09:55.920
Not only in the entrepreneurship process,

00:09:55.920 --> 00:09:58.320
but just growing as a person
to really believe in myself.

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:03.320
- Dan Egan, this man is incredibly
empowering and dedicated,

00:10:04.788 --> 00:10:08.093
and inspiring, and Jenny Tsori,

00:10:10.050 --> 00:10:13.830
just a mind-boggling
endless well of wisdom

00:10:13.830 --> 00:10:15.540
and depth and humanity.

00:10:15.540 --> 00:10:17.220
- Now that I'm in quarantine,
one thing I realized

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:21.730
I took for granted are the
events and activities at Yale.

00:10:21.730 --> 00:10:24.210
- Two things: the first
one is just the beauty

00:10:24.210 --> 00:10:28.070
of the campus itself, yeah, the buildings.

00:10:28.070 --> 00:10:31.010
And the second one is all the
people and how I could just

00:10:31.010 --> 00:10:33.170
bop around from common room to common room

00:10:33.170 --> 00:10:34.580
and hang out with everyone.

00:10:34.580 --> 00:10:36.060
I'm really missing everyone right now.

00:10:36.060 --> 00:10:39.100
- [Guy] The energy of walking
around and having people

00:10:39.100 --> 00:10:41.440
that you can just run into
on the street and have

00:10:41.440 --> 00:10:43.960
a conversation with is
something that I've really

00:10:43.960 --> 00:10:45.110
taken for granted.

00:10:45.110 --> 00:10:48.460
- Just the simple joy of
walking place to place

00:10:48.460 --> 00:10:51.440
and seeing people you know
passing by while you're walking

00:10:51.440 --> 00:10:54.330
across Old Campus, across
campus, or up Hill House.

00:10:54.330 --> 00:10:56.460
- Just being able to
run into anyone's rooms

00:10:56.460 --> 00:10:59.480
and see what friends are
around and wanna hang out.

00:10:59.480 --> 00:11:01.080
- What's something you
wish you would've done

00:11:01.080 --> 00:11:02.680
before leaving campus?

00:11:02.680 --> 00:11:04.760
I wish I walked up Hill
House one last time.

00:11:04.760 --> 00:11:07.440
- I wish my band could've
played one more time.

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:11.450
I'm a singer-songwriter and I
just started performing live

00:11:11.450 --> 00:11:14.650
this year and we thankfully
got to perform at Cafe Nine

00:11:14.650 --> 00:11:17.000
at the end of January
and that was very nice.

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:20.170
- One last swing on the
Berkley North Court tire swing.

00:11:20.170 --> 00:11:22.990
- Going to Da Legna's Pizza
because I heard that it

00:11:22.990 --> 00:11:26.420
was amazing and I had made
plans to go after Spring Break

00:11:26.420 --> 00:11:27.870
and I never made it there.

00:11:27.870 --> 00:11:29.540
But I will make it there one day.

00:11:29.540 --> 00:11:32.390
- I wish that I would've
had more of a real chance

00:11:32.390 --> 00:11:34.150
to say bye to all my best friends.

00:11:34.150 --> 00:11:36.620
I wish that we could've given
each other our last hugs,

00:11:36.620 --> 00:11:40.710
had those last meaningful
conversations in person.

00:11:40.710 --> 00:11:44.080
- I wish I would've had one
more late night conversation

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:45.793
with my suite.

00:11:45.793 --> 00:11:47.730
I think we had a lot of
those and they would last

00:11:47.730 --> 00:11:50.510
really long and they were so
fun and I wish we could've

00:11:50.510 --> 00:11:51.343
had one more.

00:11:51.343 --> 00:11:53.440
- I wish I could just
remember and say goodbye

00:11:53.440 --> 00:11:56.690
to like one last normal day.

00:11:56.690 --> 00:11:58.100
Doesn't even have to be a perfect day,

00:11:58.100 --> 00:12:00.531
just like one last normal day.

00:12:00.531 --> 00:12:03.114
(upbeat music)

00:12:21.205 --> 00:12:24.290
- I think the most important
thing that I've learned at Yale

00:12:24.290 --> 00:12:28.210
is just the variety and
the ways that people like

00:12:28.210 --> 00:12:32.750
live their lives and how they've
gotten to where they are.

00:12:32.750 --> 00:12:37.750
- I've just learned about so
many different ways of life.

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:42.860
- How to deal with really
unbelievably difficult times,

00:12:42.980 --> 00:12:45.290
at least the ones I've faced so far.

00:12:45.290 --> 00:12:46.150
It's taught me grit.

00:12:46.150 --> 00:12:47.912
- How to prioritize my
relationships and how to really

00:12:47.912 --> 00:12:49.540
be there for my friends.

00:12:49.540 --> 00:12:52.740
- How to stop procrastinating.

00:12:52.740 --> 00:12:56.430
I just became a lot better at
doing things ahead of time.

00:12:56.430 --> 00:12:57.500
- Just do your best.

00:12:57.500 --> 00:13:01.150
Don't compare yourself to
others and just take pride

00:13:01.150 --> 00:13:02.930
in your own accomplishments.

00:13:02.930 --> 00:13:05.180
- I wanna say thank you to my suite,

00:13:05.180 --> 00:13:07.860
to all of Pearson College, to
the Yale Ballroom Dance team,

00:13:07.860 --> 00:13:10.590
for really giving me a home at Yale.

00:13:10.590 --> 00:13:13.180
- Thank you to Sinim for
being the absolute best

00:13:13.180 --> 00:13:14.700
senior year roommate.

00:13:14.700 --> 00:13:16.550
You were there for me in my lows.

00:13:16.550 --> 00:13:18.513
You celebrated with me in my highs.

00:13:20.209 --> 00:13:22.440
I'm so grateful to you, so thank you.

00:13:22.440 --> 00:13:25.390
- Thank you to the Whiffenpoofs
for making me a part

00:13:25.390 --> 00:13:26.683
of the Class of 2020.

00:13:27.930 --> 00:13:28.763
It's great.

00:13:28.763 --> 00:13:32.410
- Thank you to the Class of
2019 and the Class of 2020

00:13:32.410 --> 00:13:36.350
for being my original and
current communities at Yale.

00:13:36.350 --> 00:13:38.710
- I would like to thank
Yale for pushing me outside

00:13:38.710 --> 00:13:40.920
my comfort zone and really
challenging me to think

00:13:40.920 --> 00:13:43.120
about what is it that
I'm passionate about.

00:13:43.120 --> 00:13:47.735
- Thank you to Professor
Chewess for taking the time

00:13:47.735 --> 00:13:52.110
to help me understand
so many different things

00:13:52.110 --> 00:13:56.620
that I hadn't known before
about the communities and really

00:13:56.620 --> 00:14:00.810
just giving her all for
every single student she has.

00:14:00.810 --> 00:14:04.670
- Thank you to Professor
Tracy, who taught my

00:14:04.670 --> 00:14:07.590
Writing for Television class
for teaching my favorite class

00:14:07.590 --> 00:14:11.480
that I've ever taken at Yale
and making me realize as I went

00:14:11.480 --> 00:14:14.380
through the semester that
I would love to end up

00:14:14.380 --> 00:14:15.700
in television at some point.

00:14:15.700 --> 00:14:19.010
- Thank you to all my friends
for the endless memories

00:14:19.010 --> 00:14:19.950
that we made.

00:14:19.950 --> 00:14:24.520
I miss you guys all so
much, and thank you to Yale

00:14:24.520 --> 00:14:27.660
for my bright college years.

00:14:27.660 --> 00:14:29.160
We almost made it to four.

00:14:29.160 --> 00:14:30.910
- Thank you to Yale for everything.

00:14:31.874 --> 00:14:34.457
(upbeat music)

